class Base {
protected:
    virtual void foo(); // Virtual function declaration
};

// Compiler generates a vtable for 'Base' with pointers to 'foo'
// Objects of 'Base' contain a hidden pointer to the vtable, typically at the start of the object layout.

// Constructor sequence:
// 1. Memory for object is allocated.
// 2. Base class constructor runs, initializing vtable pointer.
// 3. Derived class constructor runs, possibly overriding vtable entries.

// Destructor sequence:
// 1. Derived class destructor runs.
// 2. Base class destructor runs.
// 3. Vtable pointers are cleaned up accordingly.

// Inheritance internals:
// - Multiple inheritance may involve multiple vtable pointers.
// - Virtual inheritance introduces shared vtable structures and offset adjustments.

// Compiler optimizations:
// - Vtable placement in read-only segments.
// - Devirtualization where possible.
// - Inlining virtual calls when the target is known.
// - Object layout adjustments for cache efficiency.
